This is the sharpness statement — mathematically: if a constant C satisfies ∫|f|² ≤ C∫|f'|² for ALL such f, then C ≥ ((b−a)/2π)² since sin attains equality. To state it rigorously in Lean: 
```
/-- The constant in `poincare_wirtinger` is optimal: any real C with
`∫₀^{2π}‖f‖² ≤ C·∫₀^{2π}‖f'‖²` for every admissible f must satisfy `1 ≤ C` on the
unit-length-normalized circle, witnessed by the sine wave for which both sides equal π. -/
theorem poincare_constant_sharp
    {C : ℝ} (hC : ∀ (f f' : ℝ → ℂ), (∀ x, x ∈ [[(0 : ℝ), 2 * π]] → HasDerivAt f (f' x) x) →
      f (2 * π) = f 0 → fourierCoeffOn (by norm_num) f 0 = 0 →
      IntervalIntegrable f' volume 0 (2 * π) →
      MemLp f 2 (volume.restrict (Ioc 0 (2 * π))) →
      MemLp f' 2 (volume.restrict (Ioc 0 (2 * π))) →
      ∫ x in (0 : ℝ)..(2 * π), ‖f x‖ ^ 2 ≤ C * ∫ x in (0 : ℝ)..(2 * π), ‖f' x‖ ^ 2) :
    ((2 * π - 0) / (2 * π)) ^ 2 ≤ C := by
  have h := hC (fun x => (Real.sin x : ℂ)) (fun x => (Real.cos x : ℂ)) ...
  have hsine : ∫ ... sin ... = π := ...
  have hcos : ∫ ... cos ... = π := ...
  rw [hsine, hcos] at h
  -- π ≤ C * π → 1 ≤ C (π > 0)
  nlinarith [Real.pi_pos]
```
nlinarith with π > 0: from π ≤ C·π: 0 ≤ (C−1)·π → C ≥ 1 — nlinarith handles ✓.

